  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Reply one is the string \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\". Reply two is the string \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{\\\"d\\\":4}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\". For reply one, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\"b\":2}. For reply two, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\"d\":4}. The check gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"c\":3} as the recovered object, because the fenced candidate containing two objects fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the check falls through to the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\"c\":3} in both replies. What would make me wrong is an observation that the check returns {\"b\":2} for reply one and {\"d\":4} for reply two, or that the rule is read as not requiring the last object inside a fence when the fence contains more than one object.",
